\pard\fi0\li0\qc\ri0\sb0\sa240\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs24 VII (Bl. 100-114)
\par \pard\fi0\li0\ql\ri0\sb0\sa240\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\cf1 100\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24\cf1 r\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\cf1 -105\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24\cf1 v\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\cf1  \plain \f1\b\lang1033\fs24\cf1 Aelius Donatus: Ars grammatica (Auszug)
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx4605\ql\ri0\sb0\sa240\itap0 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 Cum barbarismo et soloecismo uicia xii nominantur\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  (!, recte numerantur) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 barbarismus, soloecismus, acirologia, cacenfaton, pleonasmos, peris\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 [s]\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 ologia, macrologia, tautologia, ecclipsis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  (!), \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 tapinosis, cacosinteton, amphibolia\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  \u8230 \'85 \endash  \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 deterrentis, ut\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  \u8218 \'82\plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 at non sic frigidus\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  (!, recte Phrygius) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 penetrat prouincia Frigia Lacedemonia pastor Lede, id est Alexander, amque\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  (= Ledaeamque) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 Helenam Troianas uexit ad urbes\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 \lquote  (Vergilius, Aeneis, lib. 7, v. 363). \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 In hoc Frigii, id est Troiani uiri, scilicet Alexander, Paris, Troianvm eneam deterrent neque temere agat vt ille Alexander Helenam rapiendo\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  (lib. 3, cap. 3-6, \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 Keil\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 , S. 394-402). \endash  (105\plain \f1\super\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 v\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  Z. 25) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 Barbarismus est una pars uiciosa in communi sermones, in poemate metaplasmus. Quare ipsum uicium pars orationis definit, cum aliud fit vicium et aliud pars orationis\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  \u8230 \'85 \endash  \u8230 \'85 \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 ideoque pars orationis recte ipsum uicivm translatiue\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18  (?) \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 definit et una pars o\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 [rationis] \plain \f1\i\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 ipsum soloecismvm, qui non vnius sed in contextu partium est\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 || Wohl Textabbruch (vgl. lib. 3, cap. 1-2, \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 Keil\plain \f1\lang1033\fs24\chcbpat8\cf18 , S. 392-393 Z. 7).
\par \pard\fi0\li0\tx4605\ql\ri0\sb0\sa195\itap0 \plain \f1\lang1033\fs20 Auszug aus lib. 3, cap. 3-6, 1-2 (Reihenfolge der Kapitel vertauscht). Gegen\u252 \'fcber der Edition von Keil interpolierte Fassung. \endash  Edition: Probi, Donati, Servii qui feruntur de arte grammatica libri, rec. Heinrich \plain \f1\scaps\lang1033\fs20 Keil\plain \f1\lang1033\fs20 . Leipzig 1864. ND Hildesheim 1961 (Grammatici latini 4).}

